Subject: [PATCH] fix CGP-4qc2-x2g2-q32h: use SecureRandom in
 RandomStringUtils/RandomUtils

RandomStringUtils and RandomUtils (CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically
Weak PRNG) generate all default random values through
java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom. The private random() factory
method in each class (RandomStringUtils.java, RandomUtils.java)
returned ThreadLocalRandom.current(), and every public generation
method in both classes -- randomAlphanumeric(), randomAlpha(),
randomNumeric(), randomAscii(), random(int), nextBoolean(),
nextBytes(), nextInt(), nextLong(), nextFloat(), nextDouble(), and
all of their overloads, including the (int,int) range overloads that
route through RandomUtils.nextInt(min,max) -- delegated to this
factory. ThreadLocalRandom is a high-throughput, non-cryptographic
PRNG whose 64-bit internal state can be reconstructed from a small
number of observed outputs, so any value it produces is predictable
to an attacker who has observed prior output. Downstream applications
that misuse these classes to generate session identifiers,
password-reset tokens, CSRF tokens, API keys, or other
security-sensitive values are therefore exposed to token forgery,
session hijacking, and password-reset or CSRF bypass.

The fix replaces the private random() factory in both
RandomStringUtils and RandomUtils with a shared, self-seeded
java.security.SecureRandom instance (a static final field, so
construction happens once, eagerly, and any construction failure
propagates rather than silently falling back to a weaker source).
This closes the single chokepoint each class funnels its default
random draws through, so the fix covers every public entry point
that does not accept a caller-supplied java.util.Random -- including
the chained (int,int) range overloads -- without changing any public
signature, thrown-exception behavior, or output length/charset
semantics. RandomStringUtils.random(count, start, end, letters,
numbers, chars, java.util.Random) and its call-through overloads,
which let a caller supply their own Random for reproducible output,
are left untouched by design.

The regression test org.apache.commons.lang3.WeakPrngExploitTest
asserts, via reflection on the private random() factories, that the
runtime type returned is java.security.SecureRandom rather than
ThreadLocalRandom for both RandomStringUtils and RandomUtils; it also
demonstrates that on the unpatched build an attacker who recovers a
thread's ThreadLocalRandom state can forge, byte-for-byte, the output
of RandomStringUtils.randomAlphanumeric(), RandomUtils.nextBytes(),
and the chained RandomStringUtils.randomAlphanumeric(min,max) route,
and that this forgery is no longer possible once SecureRandom backs
the default source. A positive control in the same test class
confirms the caller-supplied-Random overload keeps producing
identical output for a fixed-seed Random and a caller-defined Random
subclass, preserving that documented, intentional behavior.

